In-vehicle human–machine interface guidelines for augmented reality head-up displays: A review, guideline formulation, and future research directions
Putra A.R. Yamin a,b, Jaehyun Park a,c,*, Hyun K. Kim d
a Department of Industrial & Management Engineering, Incheon National University (INU), South Korea b Department of Industrial Engineering, Bandung Institute of Technology, Indonesia c Center for Perception and Behavioral Research, Incheon National University (INU), South Korea d School of Information Convergence, Kwangwoon University, South Korea

ARTICLE INFO
Keywords: Augmented reality head-up display Intelligent vehicle systems Human-machine interface guideline Ergonomic design

ABSTRACT
Background: The augmented reality head-up display (AR-HUD) recently emerged as a state-of-theart in-vehicle display. However, no comprehensive guidelines apply to the human–machine interface for AR-HUD. Objective: We formulated guidelines for the human-vehicle interface to develop an AR-HUD. Methods: A review of existing literature on AR-HUD and applicable human factors and human­ –computer interaction guidelines was conducted using the Preferred Reporting Items for Sys­ tematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses (PRISMA) approach. In total, 121 articles were selected, and an initial set of guidelines for developing an AR-HUD was drawn up. We analyzed AR-HUD development and categorized the articles based on HMI features relevant to in-vehicle ARHUD: virtual image distance, field of view, and brightness. Furthermore, research findings on projected information and legibility were investigated. Results: Nine guidelines were devised for developing the AR-HUD human–machine interface. This study also compared the traditional AR-HUD and windshield AR-HUD, defining the requirements for future studies using the term AR-HUD and providing a framework for AR-HUD design. Conclusions: Existing research on AR-HUD is limited. Future research is needed on virtual image distance, field of view, brightness, and legibility. The findings of this study could be used to guide the design of a full-windshield AR-HUD interface and future research.

1. Introduction
Augmented-reality head-up display (AR-HUD) is an advanced in-vehicle display technology that aims to provide drivers with a safer and better driving experience (Gabbard et al., 2019). The AR-HUD technology has been around for a while, but recent de­ velopments have made it more practical, accessible, and efficient. In recent years, there has been a significant increase in the adoption of AR-HUD technology in cars due to the many benefits it offers. AR-HUD utilizes real-time sensor information and progressions in internet-of-things and autonomous systems, navigational prompts, and advanced driver assistance systems (ADAS) and then presents information into the driver’s field of view (Abdi & Meddeb, 2018; Topliss et al., 2019). Therefore, it can increase attention to cued
