While traditional AR-HUDs provide drivers with digital information overlaid onto their line of sight, new full-windshield AR-HUDs take this technology a step further by projecting information across the entire windshield. This means that drivers can see digital information not only in a small section of their field of view but also in peripheral vision, providing a more immersive and natural driving experience. Full windshield AR-HUDs often use advanced artificial intelligence and machine learning algorithms to analyze data from various sensors and cameras to provide drivers with real-time information about their surroundings, such as weather conditions, traffic, and potential hazards (Murugan et al., 2022). This technology has the potential to greatly enhance driver safety and convenience by providing a more complete and informative view of the driving environment.
One significant development in AR-HUD technology is the increased use of advanced sensors that detect environmental conditions, such as rain, snow, and fog, to enhance the driver’s safety (Zhang et al., 2021). AR-HUD displays can now adapt to changing weather conditions and display the necessary information on the windshield. Secondly, AR-HUD technology can also display navigation in­ formation, such as turn-by-turn directions, lane guidance, and speed limits, helping drivers stay on track and avoid accidents. Thirdly, another development in AR-HUD technology is the use of advanced algorithms and machine learning to analyze and interpret data collected from various sensors (Zhan et al., 2020). Drivers are met with numerous elements requiring their attention while driving. The present research focuses on selected visual and cognitive distractions faced by the driver and their influence on detecting and perceiving changes in the traffic environment. The main findings show that thinking about personal problems, chores, and errands, as well as roadside advertisements on the cognitive side and looking at advertisements and the natural environment on the visual side, present the most negative impacts on drivers’ perception of crucial changes in the traffic environment. On the other hand, drivers who visually focus on traffic signals and pedestrians and think about driving speed, driving rules, and other traffic participants tend to notice crucial changes in the traffic environment more often (Cvahte Ojsterˇsek & Topolˇsek, 2019). With these algorithms, AR-HUD displays can now provide drivers more accurate and personalized information. For instance, it can learn the driver’s behavior and adjust the display accordingly. This means that the display can be customized to show only important information to aid the driver in a safe manner.
Finally, the development of AR-HUD technology has also seen the integration of various features, such as gesture recognition and voice commands (Zhou & Yin, 2021). With these features, drivers can interact with the display without taking their hands off the wheel or their eyes off the road. The gesture recognition feature allows drivers to control the display by simply waving their hands, while the voice command feature allows them to give commands and receive responses audibly. These features make AR-HUD displays more intuitive and easier to use, further enhancing the driving experience.
The AR-HUD is no longer just an option to showcase the technology and luxury of the car manufacturer. With the increase in vehicles and pedestrians and our complex lifestyle, drivers need to be aware of more objects and information on the road and respond to more unpredictable situations. AR-HUD has the potential to become common equipment in all types of vehicles and will effectively contribute to the safety of drivers and pedestrians.
To be defined as AR-HUD, unlike traditional HUDs, the augmented image should overlay real-world objects like other cars, pe­ destrians, traffic lights, etc. (Yontem et al., 2021). The purpose is to increase the driving experience and driver safety by providing images that make it easier for the driver to attend to lanes, traffic signs, and other vehicles or objects (Calvi et al., 2020b). However, displaying excessive information to the driver could result in information overload (Eyraud et al., 2015; Naujoks et al., 2015). AR applications might distract drivers, leading them to take their eyes off the road or engage them in other tasks (Kim & Gabbard, 2019; Valimont et al., 2007). To prevent distraction, an entire windshield AR-HUD interface design should be guided by human factors display design and human–computer interaction principles (Merenda et al., 2018; Park & Park, 2019). Park and Park (2019) comprehensively reviewed functional requirements for traditional automotive HUDs. However, to our best knowledge, no guidelines apply specifically for designing AR-HUD human–machine interfaces to designing AR-HUD human–machine interfaces (HMIs). This article is an extension of (Yamin et al., 2021), which was a preliminary study toward formulating guidelines for in-vehicle AR-HUD design. This article conveyed a systematic review from an updated list of references, which resulted in a more complete and rigorous review.
Briefly, the objectives of this study are the following: (1) To provide a systematic overview of existing research associated with the human-vehicle interface for AR-HUD. (2) Formulate guidelines for the human-vehicle interface to develop AR-HUD. (3) Differentiate traditional versions of AR-HUD with windshield AR-HUD. (4) Provide an AR-HUD design conceptual framework (5) Outline the key areas where future research on AR-HUD is needed.
A review of existing literature on AR-HUD was conducted to map out potential future experiments. The research methodology is provided in Section 2. Section 3 develops the results and discussion of the current research in AR-HUD HMI. Furthermore, section 4 provides the proposed HMI guidelines for AR-HUD. Subsequently, in section 5, a debate on AR-HUD terminology, conceptual framework, implications, applications, and future research is included. Finally, the conclusion is presented in section 6.

2. Method

This study conducted a comprehensive literature review following the revised PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses) method (Liberati et al., 2009), a well-accepted methodology for conducting and reporting systematic reviews in any engineering domain. Articles published from 2004 to 2024 were reviewed. The online databases utilized were Web of Science Core Collection and Science Direct. The year 2004 was chosen to ensure the comprehensiveness, timeliness, and relevance of the results in making a new set of guidelines.

