The search was conducted using a complete dual review approach (Stoll et al., 2019). Then, a third researcher reviewed the identification and screening process. This ensured all relevant studies were included in this review. A wide range of documents, including journal articles, conference papers, technical reports, and relevant guidelines, were included in the search scope. The search strings selected for this literature search were (automotive OR car OR driving) AND ((augmented AND reality) AND “head-up display”). Further exploration of topically related and interchangeable terms was conducted, shown in Table 1. The rationale for using these variants of the term “AR-HUD” is that these phrases are used interchangeably in the literature.
Papers whose abstract mention an analysis of augmented reality interface design for vehicles as part of the results are included for further review. Papers that contain information regarding the interface design requirement for AR devices were included. With the objective of deriving a comprehensive guideline for AR-HUD HMI, relevant existing guidelines and reports were included in this study. For example, the development of design and engineering recommendations for in-vehicle alphanumeric messages (SAE J2831_201204) was included in this review. The studies not written in English and articles not available for retrieval were excluded from this review. All selected articles’ content was analyzed, and the independent variables, dependent variables (if applicable), and results were compiled.
After reviewing the literature, information related to HMI for AR-HUD could be categorized into the development of in-vehicle ARHUD, virtual image distance (VID), field-of-view (FoV), brightness, projected information, and legibility. Some articles provided in­ formation on more than one category. Some articles only had one. For example, studies that developed and evaluated a certain technology or prototype regarding AR-HUD are categorized under the development of in-vehicle AR-HUD. VID refers to an AR-HUD system’s apparent distance between the projected image and the driver’s eyes. It is a critical factor in determining the visual comfort and effectiveness of the AR-HUD display. Field of view (FoV) is the extent of the environment visible to the driver through the AR-HUD system. It represents the angle of the display’s projection that covers the driver’s visual field—projected information regarding what and how to present in certain conditions. Projected information regards the type of AR information needed in certain situations. Legibility refers to the ease with which drivers can read and understand the information displayed on the screen while driving.

3. Systematic literature review

The process of identification via databases is presented in Fig. 1 below. As a result, 146 and 328 documents were obtained from Web of Science Core Collection and Science Direct, respectively, totaling 474 records identified from the search. After examining each title, 107 documents listed from the Web of Science database were found in Science Direct. Hence, these 107 duplicate records were removed. Papers whose abstract mention an analysis of augmented reality interface design as part of the results are included for further review. Papers that contain information regarding the interface design requirement for AR-HUD were included. A further 214 articles irrelevant to AR-HUD for automotive and 18 not available in English were excluded, resulting in 135 articles sought for retrieval. Ten articles were unavailable to be retrieved, and four more were excluded due to being written by the same authors but having very similar results to those of another published paper. These excluded articles were conference papers published before an extended version of the research in a journal paper. As a result, 121 critical articles relevant to this study were selected in this review. The number of articles based on its type was 74 journal papers, 40 conference proceedings, two technical reports, and five Human Factors and Human-Computer Interaction Guidelines.
