3.1. In-vehicle HMI development for AR-HUDs
In recent years, significant developments have occurred in the field of AR-HUD, with advancements in technology driving the creation of new and innovative applications. In 2013, Kim et al. created a simulation of an aftermarket HUD that presented semitransparent AR forward collision warning and blind spot warning via a virtual display with a 24 × 8◦ field of view (Kim et al., 2013). The study shows that head-up AR display interfaces for crash warning systems have potential safety benefits and a high likelihood of driver acceptance. Schall et al. also simulated an entire windshield HUD for AR collision warning in the same year, using virtual boxes integrated into the driving scene to highlight road hazards (Schall et al., 2014). The study concluded that AR cues promise to improve elderly driver safety by increasing hazard detection likelihood without interfering with other driving tasks, such as maintaining safe headway. Lauber and Butz (2013) simulated a head-up display using an LCD and combiner mirror with 70 % transparency, comparing the screen-fixed presentation of speed, speed limit, collision warning, and basic navigation information to the head-worn AR presentation. The results showed that when using head-stabilized visualizations, the content can be displayed to drivers when they are distracted or turning their heads away from the road. For safety, depending on which direction the user turns their head,

