the field of view, and brightness when designing HUD systems. The authors also review research on the effects of HUDs on driver performance, including studies on visual attention, reaction time, and driving behavior. They conclude that HUDs can potentially improve driver safety and comfort, but that further research is needed to optimize their design and evaluate their impact on driver performance in real-world driving conditions.
In an AR-HUD system, a transparent display panel projects virtual images of various driving-related information such as speed, navigation directions, and alerts onto the vehicle’s windshield. The virtual image distance is between the driver’s eyes and the pro­ jected virtual image, which appears floating in space. According to Li et al., 2019, virtual image distance should be carefully controlled to avoid visual discomfort or distortion of the virtual images. The authors suggest that the optimal virtual image distance in an AR-HUD system should be between 2 and 8 m, depending on the specific design of the display system and the driving environment. However, Texas Instruments reported that based on their study, the VID requirement is at least 6 m (Texas Instruments, 2021). The previous report by the same company (Firth, 2019) stated that 7 m is the minimum. However, it was revised to 6 m. According to Texas In­ strument’s 2021 report, the most suitable VID is 12 to 15 m. More than 15 m, any slight deviation of the augmented graphic from this range will result in less significant benefits beyond this distance.
Broy et al. (2014) explored the design parameters for a 3D HUD. They found that 3D head-up displays can provide better depth perception and improved safety, but design parameters must be optimized for optimal performance. Lisle et al. (2019) investigated the effects of volumetric augmented reality displays on human depth perception using a visual matching task. The study finds that volumetric augmented reality displays can negatively affect depth perception, particularly for tasks that require precise depth judg­ ments. Different visual parameters, such as FoV and eye position, can affect the perception of perspective in augmented reality head-up displays (Bremers et al., 2021) and cognitive tunneling (Pullukat et al., 2020). Lv et al. (2021) presented a multi-plane augmented reality head-up display system that uses volume holographic optical elements with a large area. The proposed system can provide multiple depth planes and achieve a wide field of view. A new holographic waveguide AR-HUD that is optimized for volume and does not require mirrors was presented by Richter et al. (2018). The proposed display was found to provide high image quality, a wide field of view, and reduced system complexity compared to traditional head-up displays. User satisfaction was measured with this tech­ nology for traffic information applications (Abdi et al., 2015; Wang et al., 2018).
According to Gabbard et al. (2019), the integration of graphics directly into the driving scene through computer graphics or video does not provide the same accommodative and cognitive switching capabilities as a separate AR display. This is an essential consideration for research that aims to accurately investigate AR-HUDs’ effects on a driver’s visual attention. In addition, homemade AR-HUDs, such as those created using tablets and semi-transparent combiners, may be susceptible to visual artifacts like ghosting that can impact the results of user studies unless the construction is executed with extreme care. Another issue that needs to be noted is frequent context and focal distance switching can impair driver performance (Gabbard et al., 2019).
Ren et al. (2016) evaluated the effectiveness of wide-FoV augmented reality displays in a mixed-reality simulation of driving scenarios. The wide-FoV AR display was found to improve driving performance and decrease response time to critical events. They suggested a wide field of view of at least 10◦ is needed to present pedestrians and buildings. A wider FoV in AR-HUD can enhance driver awareness and safety by providing more information about the driving environment (Kim, Isleib, et al., 2016; Kim, Miranda Anon, et al., 2016). However, an excessively wide FoV can also result in visual clutter and distraction for the driver. Therefore, the FoV should be carefully designed and calibrated to balance the amount of information presented to the driver and the clarity of the display (Gao et al., 2019).
In conclusion, the optimal FoV for AR-HUD systems depends on various factors, such as the vehicle’s design, the driving envi­ ronment, and the driver’s preferences. The study suggests that the FoV of around 12 degrees horizontal and 6 degrees vertical is appropriate for automobile AR-HUD systems (Ren et al., 2016). Lastly, Xu et al. (2023) proposed a fully connected neural network (HUDNet) for an automotive AR-HUD to correct dynamic distortion. A parallel prediction framework was developed for the image’s center and edge, and the distortion information was separated from the disparity information to balance the unstable central field of view error.

3.3. Projected information needed

Lee et al. (2016) introduced an automotive augmented reality 3D head-up display based on light-field rendering and eye-tracking. The study found that simpler displays led to fewer glances and improved driver performance than more complex displays, as in Lagoo et al. (2019). Multiple studies investigating safe glance durations for AR-HUD have been conducted (de Oliveira Faria, 2020; de Oliveira Faria et al., 2020; de Oliveira Faria & Gabbard, 2020; Eyraud et al., 2015; Genç et al., 2016; Kim & Gabbard, 2019; Topliss et al., 2020). Based on these papers, it is crucial not to present too much on the windshield, which could distract the driver.
The following studies provide information on different types of information that should or should not be projected on the wind­ shield. Yoon et al. (2014) conducted a study that describes the development of an augmented in-vehicle navigation system for a HUD. The results show that the system can improve navigation accuracy and reduce driver distraction, making it suitable for in-vehicle use. Yoon et al. later proposed an assessment model for the perceived visual complexity of automotive instrument clusters (2015a) and a method for registering AR information with a head-up display (2015b). The results suggest that the proposed model can be used to optimize the design of instrument clusters for better user experience and safety. The model also improved the accuracy and consistency of AR information display in head-up displays. Bauerfeind et al. (2022) conducted a driving simulator study to examine glance behavior regarding attention allocation while participants navigated in an ambiguous left turn situation with an oncoming car in an urban area. Results showed that with the AR display, participants paid more attention to an oncoming vehicle in the ambiguous left turn situation than the HUD, indicating that distraction due to AR information was minimal. Similarly, Chauvin et al. (2023)

