Bolton et al. (2015) studied the effects of AR presentations of landmark-based navigation using a HUD. AR landmark-based navigation was found to be more accurate and less distracting than traditional navigation. Bauerfeind et al. (2021) also investi­ gated the effects of AR on drivers’ mental load while navigating. They concluded that AR navigation could reduce drivers’ cognitive load by providing more natural and intuitive cues.
Calvi et al., (2020a) studied the effectiveness of AR warnings on driving behavior while approaching pedestrian crossings. They confirmed that augmented reality warnings significantly improved drivers’ attention and reduced driving speed when approaching pedestrian crossings. They also evaluated augmented reality cues to improve the safety of left turn maneuvers in a connected envi­ ronment via a driving simulator study (Calvi et al., 2020b). This study concluded that AR cues could significantly improve the safety of left turn maneuvers in a connected environment by providing better situational awareness to the driver.
Pathivada and Perumal (2019) analyzed drivers’ behavior at signalized intersections under mixed traffic conditions. The study identified factors influencing drivers’ decision-making at signalized intersections and proposed strategies to improve intersection safety. Li et al. (2019) also investigated the visual scanning behavior of drivers at intersections. The study was conducted in China and used naturalistic driving data. Drivers spend less time scanning the surrounding environment at signalized intersections than nonsignalized ones.
Cvahte Ojsterˇsek and Topolˇsek (2019) examined how drivers’ attention affects their ability to perceive changes in the traffic environment. The results show that visual and cognitive attention are important factors in detecting changes in the traffic environment and that cognitive load can negatively affect drivers’ perception. Li et al. (2020) proposed a method to detect manual distraction of drivers using images of their hands and ears. The proposed method achieves a detection accuracy of 83.3 %. Schwarz & Fastenmeier (2018) evaluated the effectiveness of visual advisory warnings about hidden dangers using different symbols and spatial referencing methods. The results suggest that specific symbols and spatial referencing methods can reduce unnecessary warnings and increase the effectiveness of necessary warnings.

3.4. Legibility

Legibility in the human–machine interface for in-vehicle displays refers to the ease with which drivers can read and understand the information displayed on the screen while driving. As cars become more technologically advanced, the amount of information dis­ played on in-vehicle displays has increased, making legibility a crucial factor in ensuring driver safety. Legibility is affected by various factors such as font size, color, contrast, and layout, and designers must consider these factors when creating displays to ensure that they are easily readable while driving. Campbell et al. (2004) provide guidelines for designing icons and other information elements for in-vehicle displays. ISO 15008 provides specifications and test procedures for in-vehicle visual presentation systems (International Organization for Standardization, 2017). It covers visual design principles, human factors considerations, and FoV specifications, but most specifications may not apply to a full windshield AR-HUD.
Lastly, the following summaries are research regarding color legibility. Gabbard et al. (2022) proposed a color-matching method for examining the quality of color blending in AR-HUD graphics. They suggest that the AR colors of blue, green, and yellow are relatively robust, while red and brown are not. In their 2022 study, Zhong and colleagues experimented on digital characters displayed on an in-vehicle AR-HUD, using three variables: seven colors (red, green, blue, yellow, pink, yellow–red, and white), character outlines (white, grey, or none), and ambient illuminance (daytime or night). The study presented 19 stimuli to evaluate color visibility, visual fatigue, color preference, and visual intrusion. The study found that (1) yellow and green characters were most visible during the day, while red and yellow characters were most visible at night, (2) Characters with white outlines were more visible than those with grey outlines, and visibility was affected by the luminance contrast between the character and the outline (3) Non-outlined characters blended into the display background, making the luminance contrast of the character more visible. Yamin et al. (2023) investigated the effects of button color and background on the user experience of AR interfaces in the context of driving. The study found that the AR button color and background significantly affected the participants’ accuracy and response time, with the most effective AR button color to choose to be orange and the least effective button color being blue. The orange button color had the lowest task completion time on a black and moving background. The blue button color is the least recommended compared to the other colors in this study. This contrasts with Zhou and Yin’s study (2021), which suggests that using blue or green symbols can improve the usability of ARHUDs, while red symbols may have adverse effects on usability. Dou et al. (2022) quantitatively analyzed the relationship between the content color and brightness of AR-HUD. A quantitative weighted color selection model was established to reflect the tendency towards selected elements to the optimal interval, providing a solution for the content color selection in AR-HUD interface design.

4. In-vehicle HMI guidelines for AR-HUD

Based on the information extracted from the literature review result in the previous section, 9 HMI guidelines were identified. These guidelines were derived from the research results and recommended in-vehicle specifications from the 104 selected articles of this review. Several studies suggested a different specification value, for example, VID (Li et al., 2019; Texas Instrument, 2021). After reviewing the methods and discussion from each article, we propose the following in-vehicle HMI guidelines for AR-HUD.

