4.1. Virtual image distance (VID), the field of view (FoV), and brightness

Guideline #1: Virtual image distance (VID) should be at least 6 m. A critical feature of an AR-HUD is recognizing threats by directly marking them within the driver’s field of view. To achieve this purpose, the VID requirement is at least 6 m (Texas Instruments, 2021). The previous publication (Firth, 2019) stated that 7 m is the minimum. However, it was revised to 6 m. The optimal VID is from 12 m to 15 m, and the benefit of minor misalignment of the augmented graphic from longer VIDs is diminished beyond this range (Texas Instruments, 2021). Guideline #2: The Field of View (FoV) should be more than 10◦. Corresponding with VID, a larger field of view (FoV) will improve driver experience. A wide field of view of at least 10◦ is needed to present pedestrians and buildings, according to Ren et al. (2016) and Kalinina & Putilin (2020). Studies using FoV of at least 10◦ have been conducted, measuring driver performance and acceptance (Feierle et al., 2019, 2022; J. Lee et al., 2020, 2021) and driver risk perception and anxiety (Hwang et al., 2015, 2016). However, Ren et al. (2016) further discussed that the tracking time required depends on the field of view and varies depending on age. We propose a further study on different FoV and age groups and their effect on driving performance and the usability of AR-HUD. Guideline #3: 10,000 nits is the minimum brightness needed when using AR-HUD in bright daytime. AR-HUD projects graphics onto real-world objects in front of the car. Therefore, a high requirement for brightness is needed for the pictures to be seen. Based on SAE J2831 (SAE, 2012), it is recommended to have a brightness level of at least 6,000 nits to ensure visibility under different lighting conditions. In a bright ambient lighting condition, the required luminance is more than 10,000 nits (S. C. Lee et al., 2019; Y.-H. Lee et al., 2019; Zhan et al., 2020; Zhong et al., 2022). Nits are the widely used unit of luminance used by the television and display industry. It is interchangeable with the international standard unit cd/ m2. The weather conditions when driving will naturally change the required brightness of a display. Currently, the luminance needed for AR-HUD used indoors and outdoors is unclear. Research comparing these different environment settings and brightness levels should be done in the future.

4.2. Projected information needed

Guideline #4: In a general driving situation, AR-HUD should present information on speed, lane lines, distance from stop line, distance from other vehicles, and navigation cues. Other information should not be given.
In general, for a dynamic task like driving, the complexity of the AR-HUD display should be as simple as possible, and information should be presented when needed (Merenda et al., 2019; Yoon et al., 2015). Previous studies have highlighted speed, navigation cues, lane lines, pedestrian crossings (Calvi et al., 2020a), and distance from the stop line as important information (Pathivada & Perumal, 2019). Therefore, in Guideline #4, other information is suggested not to be presented to minimize the display complexity.
Guideline #5: When driving through an intersection, the AR-HUD should highlight the correct road lane and present spatial referencing on other vehicles and pedestrians.
Cognitive load is higher in intersections, especially in mixed traffic scenarios (Calvi et al., 2020b; Choudhary & Velaga, 2019; G. Li et al., 2019; Pathivada & Perumal, 2019). Vehicle type, a characteristic of mixed traffic conditions, significantly impacts the driver’s decision at the onset of yellow.
Guideline #6: When driving at high speeds, the AR-HUD should highlight the correct road lane, distance from other vehicles, and audiovisual warning messages for collision alerts.
It is also interesting to note that highways, advertisements, and the natural environment represent the visual elements that distract the driver from taking his eyes away from the relevant traffic environment (Cvahte Ojsterˇsek & Topolˇsek, 2019). Therefore, we propose that on highways and when the vehicle is driving at high speeds, AR-HUD should increase assistance to the driver to minimize the adverse outcomes of distraction.
Guideline #7: When driving after congestion, provide audiovisual warning messages for collision alerts. After congestion, it is found that driving behavior tends to be more aggressive and prone to accidents (Li et al., 2020). As a result, in Guideline #7, AR-HUD must increase assistive cues and warnings after congestion. In a navigating task experiment, using augmented landmark boxes is recommended to increase the driver’s attention to obstacles, compared to only using arrows (Bolton et al., 2015; Bauerfeind et al., 2021). On top of that, spatial referencing of an unspecific warning symbol consistently improved drivers’ reactions (Schwarz & Fastenmeier, 2017). An animating warning message increases drivers’ attention (Campbell et al., 2018). However, Schwarz and Fastenmeier (2017) found that using animation should be selected with care and only for alerting a high degree of danger.

4.3. Legibility

Guideline #8: Letter Guidelines The text size should be 12–20 arcmin for the general readers and 30 arcmins for headings. The stroke/height ratio should be 0.08––0.2 for standard size and 0.1–0.2 for small writing. The aspect/length for alphabetic characters should be 0.65–0.8. The rec­ ommended font is San Serif, with a height/outline thickness ratio 0.08. The outline thickness for small letters should be within 0.35 mm when using an outline. Shadow direction should be 25◦-155◦. Stroke/shadow thickness is recommended to be 70 %. Lastly, line spacing should be three arcmins. Relevant existing Human Factors and Human-Computer Interaction guidelines were reviewed for proposing letter and color

