guidelines. Letter guidelines comply with ISO 15008 (ISO, 2017), SAE-J2831 (2012), TRL-PA3721/01 (Stevens et al., 2002), Campbell et al. (2004), and Naujoks et al. (2019). We argue that these standards apply to AR-HUD. Nonetheless, it would be worth conducting future research to validate these legibility guidelines specifically for AR-HUD and how it affects different age groups (Pampel et al., 2018).
Guideline #9: Color guidelines Change the highlight color to red when a vehicle, bicycle, or pedestrian is less than 5 m from one’s car. Use red to indicate highly urgent messages, yellow to indicate exemplary information, and green to indicate normal operations or safe conditions. Refrain from using the combinations of green–red, green–blue, yellow–red, yellow-blue, purple-red, and blue-white. Orange is recommended for an AR button or box-shaped graphic (Yamin et al., 2023). For text color, use white outlines and refrain from using grey outlines (Zhong et al., 2022). It is recommended not to use more than four colors for semantic coding (Campbell et al., 2018). Color plays a vital role in successful interpretation. Practical uses of color can help a driver group information and code infor­ mation, attract their attention more quickly, and facilitate interpretation via population stereotypes (Campbell et al., 2018). The findings of this study’s HMI design feature, recommendation, and reference are shown in Table A2. It should be noted that only studies from Table A1 were included in the guideline formulating process, but not all studies in Table A1 were included in the guidelines and Table A2.

5. Discussion

This review article aims to derive an initial guideline on AR-HUD human–machine interfaces.

5.1. Differences between traditional AR-HUD and windshield AR-HUD
AR-HUD has been mentioned in several publications about HUDs, and we found the distinction between traditional AR-HUDs and advanced windshield AR-HUD is unclear. Therefore, defining the difference between current and future AR-HUDs and early versions of AR-HUDs is meaningful. Early versions of AR-HUD also place some information on the windshield with or without putting a sticker on the windshield. We propose categorizing these devices into traditional AR-HUD because the projected information, such as tracking pedestrians and nearby vehicles, could not interact with the real world. Based on the results found in section 4, we could distinguish that, unlike traditional AR-HUDs, where the graphics do not overlay onto real-world objects, the definition of a true AR-HUD should. Fig. 2 depicts the requirement differences between traditional AR-HUDs and windshield AR-HUDs. Fig. 3 shows a traditional AR-HUD (right) and windshield AR-HUD (left).
Another notable difference between a traditional AR-HUD and an advanced AR-HUD is where the information is placed. Traditional AR-HUDs are usually located above, behind, or left/right of the steering wheel. The further away it is from the road line of sight, the

