longer the duration of the driver’s glance at the HUD. An in-vehicle HUD is usually located 40–60 cm from the driver’s eyes, whereas earlier AR-HUD projects its image to 2–3 m in front of the driver (Maag et al., 2023). Because a conventional AR-HUD image is located near the driver, it is adequate for the field of view to be below 8◦, even less than 4◦ for most devices. A modern AR-HUD, however, should project the augmented graphic on the windshield and onto real-world objects in the driver’s line of sight while driving. This means augmented landmark boxes, lines, and text are projected on real-world objects like roads, buildings, vehicles, and pedestrians. Therefore, the driver will not need to move their eyes from the road to see the necessary information.
To accommodate all these features, the virtual image distance should be at least 6 m, with 12–15 m as the optimal distance, and the field of view should be more than 10◦ (Texas Instruments, 2021). When the VID is greater than 6 m, the eyes are less sensitive to discrepancies in physiological distance cues and will perceive that the AR information is more strongly fused with the real world.
A longer VID in an AR-HUD system can result in a better user experience and lower system cost. A longer VID will result in less eye fatigue, a negligible parallax effect when the driver moves their head or eyes, a better fusion between AR information and real-world objects, and eliminating wedge film and eye-tracking requirements. In their technical white paper, Texas Instruments (2021) states that the optimal VID is between 12 m and 15 m. In this range, virtual images are possibly fused with the real world. However, the benefit diminishes if the VID increases beyond this recommended range. It must be considered that a longer VID negatively impacts HUD systems with traditional optical designs by creating a solar load challenge. Therefore, technology that can tolerate much higher solar loads and enable long VIDs without thermal issues is needed.
The AR-HUD should also accurately mark surrounding vehicles and pedestrians in night vision/rain-fog conditions, improving driving safety in poor visibility. The brightness and contrast of the virtual image are critical to achieving this. Traditional HUDs are inside the vehicle with a still background, so the brightness level required is around 200–500 nits. However, the virtual image from

