advanced AR-HUDs is projected into the outdoor environment. Therefore, the brightness level required is the same as that required when using AR outdoors during bright daytime, which is 10,000 nits (Lee et al., 2019a, 2019b; Zhan et al., 2020).
What and when certain warning information should be presented is essential. Guidelines 4–7 discuss this. Current advanced nonAR-HUDs use audiovisual warning messages. The difference between AR-HUDs is that the image could now present augmented landmark boxes and spatial referencing, further improving the driver’s attention to target objects.

5.2. AR-HUD design conceptual framework
Park and Park (2019) reviewed the literature on head-up displays in automotive applications to identify the functional re­ quirements of such displays. The study identifies the key functional requirements of head-up displays, including visibility, readability, and usability, and provides recommendations for the design of such displays. Based on the literature review and proposed AR-HUD guidelines, a conceptual framework has been formulated to further guide the development of AR-HUD design. Fig. 4, based on Park and Park’s (2019) HUD framework, depicts the conceptual framework that shows the relationship between the design inputs, functional requirement analysis, and interface design for AR-HUD and how it affects usefulness in driving. AR-HUD design consists of available requirement analysis and interface design. The functional requirement analysis results in direct interface design, whereas interface design limits the functional requirement. Similarly, design inputs such as user characteristics, tasks, environment conditions, and technology availability (and limitation) are factors considered in AR-HUD design. Strle et al. (2023) investigated physiological indicators for modeling HUD-induced cognitive load to understand the relationship between the user’s characteristics and HUD type. Tabone et al. (2023) highlighted the importance of involving target users in design and evaluation.
Functional requirement analysis is created from information derived from functions of existing AR-HUD and mapping the user needs. It is then used to determine the AR-HUD functions which will affect utility. In contrast, interface design is informed and limited by existing Human Factors & Human-Computer Interaction design principles and guidelines. This will create the AR-HUD interface, which affects its usability. In turn, the usability of the AR-HUD interface determines the driving experience (Liang et al., 2023), driver situation awareness (see Greatbatch et al. (2022) and Mukhopadhyay et al. (2023), and driver safety (Maag et al., 2023). This is in line with numerous studies. Yontem et al. (2021) proposed HMI provides a more immersive and intuitive driving experience, improving driver safety and comfort. Calvi et al., (2020a) and Gabbard et al. (2019) also discuss the link between AR-HUD interface usability and positive driving experience (Halit et al., 2021). Through an eye-tracking study, Cheng et al. (2023) verified that AR-HUD technology can significantly improve the driver’s attention to risky Areas of Interest. It has been shown that the AR-HUD interface can reduce the difficulty of processing information in risky driving scenarios.

5.3. Key results and implications of this study
The novel contributions of this article are the following: (1) Providing a systematic overview of existing research associated with the human-vehicle interface for AR-HUD (Table A.1) (2) Formulating a set of guidelines for the human-vehicle interface to develop ARHUD (Table A.2) (3) Differentiating traditional versions of AR-HUD with windshield AR-HUD (Fig. 2) (4) Provided an AR-HUD design conceptual framework (Fig. 4) (5) Outlining the key areas where future research on AR-HUD is needed.
