following the VID, FoV, Brightness, and legibility requirements as stated in the left boxes. Only with these specifications and at critical places and times, such as intersections, passing advertisements, and after congestion, should the AR-HUD present a higher amount of information. If not, it is recommended only to show very simple information such as speed and arrow for navigation. An in-vehicle ARHUD aims to improve the driving experience and safety (Gabbard et al., 2019). The design of AR-HUD should ensure that the device aids the driver instead of increasing distraction by displaying excessive information (Naujoks et al., 2015; Eyraud et al., 2015) and is accepted and trusted by the driver (Li et al., 2022).
There is no one-size-fits-all recommendation for the human–machine interface (HMI) specification for an in-vehicle augmented reality head-up display (AR-HUD), as the optimal HMI design depends on various factors such as the specific use case, user preferences, and technological constraints. However, some general principles for designing an effective AR-HUD include ensuring that the display is well-integrated with the driver’s field of view, providing relevant and timely information, minimizing distraction and cognitive workload, and offering customizable features to cater to individual user needs. Additionally, the HMI should be designed with safety in mind, ensuring that the driver’s attention is not diverted from the road and that the display does not cause visual or cognitive overload. Ultimately, an AR-HUD HMI aims to enhance driving performance and situational awareness while minimizing the risk of accidents or errors.
The results of this study summarize essential human–machine interface features to achieve the intended benefits of AR-HUD. The study is expected to be beneficial for furthering the design of an AR-HUD-assisted system. However, there will be many challenges that need to be addressed for an AR-HUD designer and manufacturer.
First is graphics alignment or sensor fusion. It is displaying the graphics on the correct real-world object based on the vehicles’ sensor data (Chen et al., 2023). The rapid change in the environment’s movement, color, and brightness would mean the AR-HUD HMI software should be able to decide and adapt to all changes. The position of the driver’s eye box also contributes to the graphics alignment challenge, called the parallax effect. Parallax is the change of alignment of the virtual image and the real-world object when the human eye changes position. When a driver moves their head slightly to one side, the virtual image will also move and cause misalignment. Chen et al. investigated the impact of AR graphic position (peripheral AOI v.s. central AOI) and the relative position of the AR graphic on unpredictable stimuli (on-HUD hazard v.s. off-HUD hazard) on the occurrence of inattentional blindness. In the peripheral visual field, unpredictable stimuli accidentally superimposed on the AR graphic (i.e., on-HUD hazard) lead to a higher probability of ignoring the accidental events and seem to require a longer response time for drivers. The study illustrated that inat­ tentional blindness to nonaugmented stimuli is also influenced by the AR graphic position when AR technology fails to augment them promptly. An important recommendation emerging from this work is to consider the design of AR graphics according to the AR graphic positions and stimulus types to reduce the occurrence of inattentional blindness. Furthermore, Texas Instruments (2021) calculated the magnitude of misalignment compared to different VID and eye positions. When the VID is at 13 m, the misalignment is negligible.
Accommodating differing driver heights requires adjusting the eye-box size. Regardless of the type of technology used, a larger eye box comes with the expense of increased lumens. The lumens required to support a HUD design are directly proportional to the FOV and eye-box area. The greater the number of lumens, the greater the power consumption and the more challenging the thermal design becomes (Firth, 2019). Further research on developing efficient imaging technology for all users and developing the complete AR-HUD software should be done.
An AR-HUD needs to produce constant performance in brightness, color, latency, and contrast in any temperature, driving con­ dition, and power consumption. Furthermore, longer VIDs and associated higher magnification create significant solar load challenges. Traditional HUDs have optical designs with a magnification of around five times, whereas the AR-HUDs have a magnification of 25x to 30x. It is critical to choose an efficient light source and imaging technology to support the required lumen budget at minimal power consumption while considering the thermal implications. Further research should be conducted on the relationship between FoV, eyebox size, brightness level, and lumen.
Early versions of AR-HUD have a challenge with polarization. In some imaging technologies, a driver wearing polarized sunglasses could not see the HUD image. The information from the AR-HUD is essential. Therefore, advanced AR-HUD must tackle this problem and make sure all images are visible when wearing any glasses.

5.4. Limitations and future work

This study comes with the following limitations that leave room for further research. This study did not develop an AR-HUD prototype, nor did we use a commercial AR-HUD to investigate. The focus of this paper was to present a set of the most relevant guidelines that can be used for designing AR-HUD. Furthermore, this study did not consider the costs and limitations of the current technology available. This is because this review was intended to provide information on the near-optimal HMI specification and features. Advancements in technology and the widespread use of this product will eventually lower the production cost and price. Nevertheless, future studies should be conducted on the willingness to purchase and use AR-HUD.
We propose a further study on different FoV and age groups and their effect on driving performance and the usability of AR-HUD. Bellet et al. (2018) raised the issue of difficulties experienced by older drivers with advanced driving aid systems such as AR-HUD. Brightness (luminance) is compromised when the environmental luminance changes; however, the difference in brightness values required has not been studied yet. Therefore, future studies should be performed on this. Next, the luminance needed for AR-HUD used indoors and outdoors is unclear. Research comparing these different environment settings and brightness levels should be done in the future.
The following are more recommendations for future AR-HUD studies. There is a need for a study on the costs and use of AR-HUD. Second, a study that includes a control group will compare the driving performance and user experience with and without AR-HUD

