technology. Third, future experiments should incorporate a diverse range of driving scenarios, such as highway driving, urban driving, and adverse weather conditions, to assess the effectiveness of AR-HUD systems in various situations. Third, the long-term effects of using AR-HUD technology on driver behavior and safety over an extended period should be evaluated. Fourth, a comparative analysis of different AR-HUD designs and features will be conducted to determine the most effective configuration for improving driving performance and user satisfaction. Fifth, future studies should consider the impact of drivers’ age and experience level on the usability and acceptance of AR-HUD systems. Lastly, a future work should be done on validating legibility guidelines specifically for AR-HUD and how it affects different age groups (Pampel et al., 2018).

6. Conclusions

The current study derived initial guidelines for developing AR-HUD from existing literature and applicable guidelines and stan­ dards. In total, 121 key articles relevant to this study were selected in this review, consisting of 74 journal papers, 40 conference proceedings, two technical reports, and five Human Factors and Human-Computer Interaction Guidelines. The major findings of this study are the following. Firstly, we analyzed the development of AR-HUD and categorized the articles based on HMI features relevant to in-vehicle AR-HUD: virtual image distance, the field of view, and brightness. Furthermore, research findings on projected infor­ mation and legibility were investigated. Future windshield AR-HUD is recommended to be developed following the VID, FoV, brightness, and legibility requirements. Only with these specifications and at critical places and times, such as intersections, passing advertisements, and after congestion, should the AR-HUD present a higher amount of information. If not, it is recommended only to show elementary details such as speed and arrow for navigation. Secondly, an AR-HUD design conceptual framework was made based on the findings. An in-vehicle AR-HUD aims to improve the driving experience, driver’s situational awareness, and safety. The AR-HUD design should ensure that the device helps the driver, not distracts it. Thirdly, this study also compared the traditional AR-HUD and windshield AR-HUD, defining the requirements for future studies using the term AR-HUD.
It has been found that current research on AR-HUD is limited. Based on the findings, future research is needed on the following: Experiment on different VIDs to drive performance, investigate the relationship between other FoVs and other age groups to AR-HUD usability and operating performance, investigate the costs of AR-HUD technology, evaluate the long-term effects of using AR-HUD technology on driver behavior and safety over an extended period, comparing different environment settings and different bright­ ness levels, and to validate legibility guidelines specifically for AR-HUD.

